## Summary

**Task 1 — Eagle fog penalty (behavior change, as you approved).** I added the same guard Dingo uses to the top of `Eagle.findFood()`:
```java
if (getFog() && rand.nextInt(2) != 0) {
    return null;
}
```
The eagle now genuinely fails to hunt ~50% of the time in fog, consistent with the dingo. This is a deliberate behavior change — previously fog had no effect on the eagle.

**Task 2 — shared adjacent-iteration skeleton.** `spreadDisease` and `giveBirth` each carried their own copy of the same boilerplate: get the field, fetch `adjacentLocations(getLocation())`, iterate with an explicit `Iterator`, and read the occupant at each cell. I extracted that into one private helper:
```java
private List<Object> adjacentOccupants() {
    Field field = getField();
    List<Object> occupants = new LinkedList<>();
    for (Location where : field.adjacentLocations(getLocation())) {
        Object occupant = field.getObjectAt(where);
        if (occupant != null) {
            occupants.add(occupant);
        }
    }
    return occupants;
}
```
Both methods became simple enhanced-for loops over `adjacentOccupants()`, each keeping its own per-occupant logic (disease spread vs. mate check). I also flattened `giveBirth`'s four levels of nested `if` into a single combined condition, and swapped Animal's now-unused `import java.util.Iterator` for `java.util.LinkedList`.

